  "translation": {
    "literal": "A tall tree dressed up in jade-green; ten thousand twigs hang down like green silk ribbons. I do not know who has cut out these slender leaves; the spring wind of the second month is like a pair of scissors.",
    "poetic": "Dressed all in jade, the willow stands tall, its countless twigs hanging like green silk ribbons. Who cut out these slender leaves? It was the spring wind of February, like a pair of shears."
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "metaphors",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "Three metaphors and a question",
      "body": "The willow is first a beautiful woman dressed in jade (妝), then its branches become silk ribbons. Then comes a question, \"who cut these leaves?\", and the answer is a surprise: the wind itself is a pair of scissors. A cold wind that cuts becomes a warm, creative one."
    },
    {
      "id": "month",
      "kind": "other",
      "title": "Which February?",
      "body": "二月 means the second month of the lunar calendar, which falls roughly between late February and early April, the time willows come into leaf. It is early spring, not midwinter."
    },
    {
      "id": "poet",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "He Zhizhang",
      "body": "He Zhizhang (659–744) was a poet, calligrapher and one of the \"Eight Immortals of the Wine Cup\" in Du Fu's poem. He also wrote 回鄉偶書. This poem is usually the first one children learn from him."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 高 · 絛 · 刀",
      "body": "Lines one, two and four end in 高 (gāo), 絛 (tāo) and 刀 (dāo), in the 豪 rhyme (-ao). Line three ends in 出 and does not rhyme."
    }
  ],
